isWellDefined(SimplicialModuleMap) -- whether a map of simplicial modules is well-defined
*****************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note isWellDefined: (Macaulay2Doc)isWellDefined,
  * Usage: 
        isWellDefined f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note Map of Simplicial Modules: SimplicialModuleMap,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, that is true when f
        determines a well defined simplicial module map

Description
===========

A map of simplicial modules $f : C \to D$ of degree $d$ is a sequence of maps
$f_i : C_i \to D_{d+i}$.  No relationship is required between these maps and
the face/degeneracy maps in the source and target.

This routine checks that $C$ and $D$ are well-defined simplicial modules, and
that, for each $f_i$, the source and target equal $C_i$ and $D_{d+i}$,
respectively.  If the variable debugLevel is set to a value greater than zero,
then information about the nature of any failure is displayed.

Unlike the *note corresponding function for SimplicialModules:
isWellDefined_lpSimplicialModule_rp,, the basic constructors for simplicial
module maps are all but assured to be well defined. The only case that could
cause a problem is if one constructs the source or target complex, and those
are not well defined.

