map(SimplicialModule,SimplicialModule,HashTable) -- make a map of simplicial modules
************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note map: (Macaulay2Doc)map,
  * Usage: 
        f = map(D, C, H)
  * Inputs:
      * C, a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, 
      * D, a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, 
      * H, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, whose keys are
        integers, and whose values are the maps between the corresponding terms
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value null, specify the degree of a map of simplicial modules
      * *note DegreeLift: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLift=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, unused option for maps of simplicial modules
      * *note DegreeMap: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeMap=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, unused option for maps of simplicial modules
  * Outputs:
      * f, a *note Map of Simplicial Modules: SimplicialModuleMap,, 

Description
===========

A map of simplicial modules $f : C \rightarrow D$ of degree $d$ is a sequence
of maps $f_i : C_i \rightarrow D_{d+i}$. No relationship between the maps $f_i$
and the face/degeneracy maps of either $C$ or $D$ is assumed.

We construct a map of simplicial modules by specifying the individual maps
between the terms. Note that this constructor is typically used more behind the
scenes, and using the *note simplicialModule: simplicialModule, or basic map
constructors is typically much more efficient than typing the maps by hand.

