map(SimplicialModule,SimplicialModule,SimplicialModuleMap) -- make a new map of simplicial modules, induced from an existing one
********************************************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note map: (Macaulay2Doc)map,
  * Usage: 
        g = map(D, C, f)
  * Inputs:
      * C, a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, 
      * D, a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, 
      * f, a *note Map of Simplicial Modules: SimplicialModuleMap,, regarded as
        providing matrices which induce maps between the terms of $C$ and $D$
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value null, specify the degree of a map of simplicial modules
      * *note DegreeLift: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLift=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, unused option for maps of simplicial modules
      * *note DegreeMap: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeMap=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, unused option for maps of simplicial modules
  * Outputs:
      * g, a *note Map of Simplicial Modules: SimplicialModuleMap,, 

Description
===========

A map of simplicial modules $f : C' \rightarrow D'$ is a sequence of maps $f_i
: C'_i \rightarrow D'_{d'+i}$. The new map $g : C \rightarrow D$ is the
sequence of maps $g_i : C_i \rightarrow D_{d+i}$ induced by the matrix of
$f_i$.

One use for this function is to get the new map of simplicial modules induced
by forgetting the underlying complexes of the source and target, assuming that
the source and target are obtained as Dold-Kan images.

