schurMap -- construct the Dold-Puppe extension of the Schur functor
*******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        schurMap(lambda, S, Options => {Degeneracy => false, TopDegree => null})
  * Inputs:
      * lambda, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, A list representing a
        partition.
      * S, a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, or *note
        SimplicialModuleMap: SimplicialModuleMap,, or *note Complex:
        (Complexes)Complex,, or *note ComplexMap: (Complexes)ComplexMap, to
        apply the Schur functor extension.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degeneracy: schurMap_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegeneracy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => a
        *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value false,
        Default value is false. Indicates whether to include degeneracy maps in
        the construction.
      * *note TopDegree: schurMap_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmTopDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => an
        *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value null, Default is null.
        If provided, specifies the top degree for the construction of the
        simplicial module.
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, The Dold-Puppe extension
        of the Schur functor applied to the simplicial module S.

Description
===========

This function constructs the Dold-Puppe extension of the Schur functor to the
category of simplicial modules, applied to a simplicial module S or a complex
(or maps thereof). The construction involves creating a simplicial module where
each component is a Schur functor applied to the corresponding component of S.

By default, degeneracy maps are not computed in this method since it adds
additional computation time that is not necessary for computing the
normalization or many of the invariants of interest. However, if the user is
interested in having degeneracy maps, use the option Degeneracy => true.

