SimplicialModule -- the class of all simplicial modules
*******************************************************

Description
===========

A simplicial R-module is a presheaf on the so-called Simplex Category, with
values in the category of R-modules. Concretely, such objects can be viewed as
nonnegatively graded R-modules equipped with certain face and degeneracy
operators satisfying the simplicial identities. As an example, every R-module M
can be converted into a simplicial R-module whose degree n piece is equal to M
for all n, and with face/degeneracy operators simply given by the identity.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = simplicialModule(ZZ^2,3,Degeneracy => true) --the integer 3         |
|                                                                             |
|       2       2       2       2                                             |
|o1 = ZZ  <-- ZZ  <-- ZZ  <-- ZZ <-- ...                                      |
|                                                                             |
|     0       1       2       3                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : SimplicialModule                                                        |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|specifies a top degree, the option Degeneracy specifies whether or not to    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|compute degeneracy maps                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The output string for a simplicial module is meant to indicate that this object
is infinite in general, and the user can only compute a finite snapshot of the
object. The face/degeneracy maps can be accessed using the keys dd and ss,
respectively. In order to verify that the resulting face/degeneracy maps
satisfy the simplicial identities, one can use the *note isSimplicialModule:
isSimplicialModule_lpSimplicialModule_rp, command.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : keys S                                                          |
|                                                                     |
|o2 = {complexLength, module, ss, dd, ring, complex, topDegree, cache}|
|                                                                     |
|o2 : List                                                            |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+

