simplicialModule -- make a simplicial module
********************************************

  * Usage: 
        simplicialModule(H1, H2, H3, d)
  * Inputs:
      * H1, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, or *note Complex:
        (Complexes)Complex,, used to determine the modules in each degree
      * H2, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, used to specify the
        face maps
      * H3, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, optional, used to
        specify degeneracy maps if needed
      * d, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, an integer, specifying the top
        degree to compute the simplicial module up to
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degeneracy: simplicialModule_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegeneracy=>_pd_pd_pd_
        rp, => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        false, option specifying whether or not to also compute the degeneracy
        maps
      * Base (missing documentation) => ..., default value 0, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Simplicial Module: SimplicialModule,, 

Description
===========

A simplicial module is a sequence of objects (e.g. modules), connected by maps
called face/degeneracy maps denoted by $d$ and $s$, respectively. These maps
satisfy the simplicial identities: 1. For face maps: \[ d_j d_i = d_i d_{j-1}
\text{ for } 0 \leq i < j \leq n \] 2. For face and degeneracy maps: \[ d_i s_j
= s_{j-1} d_i \text{ for } i < j \] \[ d_j s_j = \text{id} \] \[ d_{j+1} s_j =
\text{id} \] \[ d_k s_j = s_j d_{k-1} \text{ for } k > j+1 \] 3. For degeneracy
maps: \[ s_j s_i = s_i s_{j+1} \text{ for } i \leq j \]

This constructor is the most basic constructor for building a simplicial
module, and is called by all of the more user friendly constructors. It is
highly recommended that the user sees *note simplicialModule(Complex): The
Dold-Kan Correspondence in Macaulay2, to quickly build simplicial modules.

